@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-exceptions Checking exceptions
@brief Functions for verifying exceptions in test cases.

@details
Three functions check what a callable throws, and are used as the
conditions of `expect()` or `require()`:
- `mt::throws (callable)` is true when the callable throws any exception;
- `mt::throws<E> (callable)` is true when it throws an exception of type
  `E`, or of a type derived from it;
- `mt::nothrow (callable)` is true when it throws nothing.

The exception is caught by the check, so it does not leave the test case.
When the check fails, the report states why, for example:
- `throws<std::runtime_error>, but nothing was thrown`;
- `throws<std::runtime_error>, but a std::exception was thrown: "what"`,
  with the text returned by its `what()`;
- `nothrow, but an exception of unknown type was thrown`.

A failed `require()` inside the callable is not an exception of the
callable: it stops the test case, as it does elsewhere, and does not
satisfy `throws()` or fail `nothrow()`.

@par Example

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check if exceptions are thrown", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (mt::throws ([] { exercise_throw (true); }))
      << "exception thrown";

  t.expect (mt::throws<std::runtime_error> ([]
    { throw std::runtime_error{ "" }; }))
      << "std::runtime_error thrown";

  t.expect (mt::nothrow ([] { exercise_throw (false); }))
      << "exception not thrown";
});
@endcode

@par Availability
The functions are declared only when the compiler defines
`__cpp_exceptions`. In a build without exceptions (for example with
`-fno-exceptions`), the code under test cannot throw, the functions do
not exist, and a test case that calls them does not compile; guard such
test cases with `#if defined(__cpp_exceptions)`.

@par Several exception types
To accept one of several exception types, use a `try` block with a
`catch` for each type, and report each outcome with `expect (true)` or
`expect (false)`. Check the normal path too, after the call in the `try`
block; otherwise a call that throws nothing passes with no check:

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check exceptions explicitly", [] (auto& t) {
  try
    {
      compute_answer ();
      t.expect (false) << "no exception thrown";
    }
  catch (const std::overflow_error& e)
    {
      t.expect (true) << "std::overflow_error thrown";
    }
  catch (const std::runtime_error& e)
    {
      t.expect (true) << "std::runtime_error thrown";
    }
  catch (...)
    {
      t.expect (false) << "unknown exception thrown";
    }
});
@endcode
